course: dictionary
lesson: 6658
title: Words that can hurt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are slang. They are informal, used in chatty talk and stories, and they are not always polite. Nutcase is one of them. It means a person who acts in a very silly, wild or crazy way.
explain: The same word can feel very different depending on who says it. Among friends, «You're a total nutcase!» can be a joke, a fun, daft way of saying someone is being silly. Said unkindly, it is an insult.
explain: Context helps you see which one it is. Look at the words around it and at how the speaker sounds. In a story, if someone uses it to tease or be rude, the speaker is being unkind. «Calling someone a "nerve-disease" case means nutcase or psycho; it mocks people with real illnesses and is not a funny word.» Real illnesses are no joke, so do not use nutcase to hurt anyone.
fact: Nutcase is a noun. You can have one nutcase or two nutcases.
fact: «Nutcase» means a person who acts in a very silly, wild or crazy way.
fact: Among friends, «nutcase» can be a fun, daft joke.
fact: Used unkindly, «nutcase» is an insult, and it is hurtful to people with mental illness.
fact: Slang is informal everyday language. Be careful with it, because a word that is funny to friends can hurt someone who hears it.
exercise: «You're a total nutcase!» said with a laugh by a friend, as a joke. What does nutcase mean here? | A person who is a doctor | *A fun, daft person | A person who is very quiet and shy | A tall tree
exercise: What is the plural of nutcase? | nutcase's | nutcaseen | nutcasies | *nutcases
exercise: Why must you be careful using nutcase about someone? | Because it means a famous singer | Because it only works in Scotland | *Because it can hurt people, especially those with real illnesses | Because it is a very long word
test: In a story, a boy shouts «Look at you, nutcase!» to a classmate who is upset. What does this tell you about the boy? | He is very hungry | He is being very kind and helpful | He is lost and needs help | *He is being unkind and teasing
test: Which use of nutcase is a friendly joke? | Said to a person with a real illness | Said angrily to someone who is crying | *Said with a grin by a friend who is laughing | Said to make someone feel left out
exam: Why is it a good idea to avoid using nutcase to hurt people? | *Because it is unkind and hurts people, especially those with real mental illness | Because it is a Scottish word | Because it is too long to say | Because it means a very clever person
